==================================================
Lab 3: File Path Traversal, Traversal Sequences Stripped Non-Recursively
==================================================

=======================
WHAT THIS LAB IS ABOUT
=======================

A shopping website loads product images through a URL that includes the filename.
The server removes ../ from the filename, but it removes it only once.
We can hide a second ../ inside the first, so one removal leaves a working traversal sequence behind.

Goal: read the contents of /etc/passwd.

==============================
HOW THE WEBSITE NORMALLY WORKS
==============================

When a product image loads, the browser requests /image?filename=<name>.
The server reads the file from its image folder, /var/www/images/, and returns it.

=======================
 WHAT IS THE PROBLEM
=======================

The filter runs a single pass over the input. It finds each ../ and deletes it, then stops.
Deleting a sequence can join the characters on either side, and those joined characters can form a new ../ that the filter never checks.

===========================
THREE THINGS TO UNDERSTAND
===========================

Traversal sequence (../): This moves up one folder. The filter tries to remove it.
Single-pass filter: It removes matches once, without checking the result again.
Joined characters: When ../ is removed from ....//, the two dots on each side join together, creating a new ../.

======================================
THE ATTACK
======================================

Instead of a normal traversal sequence, we send:

....//....//....//etc/passwd

The filter removes the inner ../ from each ....// group. Each group loses its middle ../, and the remaining characters join into a new ../.

....//  becomes  ../

So the value the server uses is:

../../../etc/passwd

The three ../ move up from /var/www/images/ to /, and the path then reaches /etc/passwd.

============================
HOW TO SOLVE IT MANUALLY
============================

Open the lab and load any product image.
Replace the filename in the image URL with ....//....//....//etc/passwd.
Load the page. The text of /etc/passwd appears, and the lab is marked as solved.

=========================
WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES
=========================

* It asks for the lab's base URL and builds the target as /image.
* It sends a GET request with filename set to ....//....//....//etc/passwd. The params argument adds it to the URL query string.
* If the server returns status 200, it prints the response text, which is the contents of /etc/passwd.
* If the request fails or the status is different, it prints an error instead.

=============================
WHY THIS HAPPENS
=============================

The filter checks the input once and assumes that removing a pattern cannot create a new one.
It can, because removing text can bring together characters that were not next to each other before.

=============================
HOW TO PREVENT THIS
=============================

Do not build file paths from user input. Map each image to an ID or a fixed name on the server.
If user input must be used, resolve the full path and check that it still starts with the allowed folder.
Do not rely on removing bad patterns. Reject the input when it contains any traversal sequence, and repeat the check until nothing changes if you must strip.

Other good practices:

Run the web server with only the file permissions it needs.
Keep sensitive files outside the paths the application can reach.

=============================
 KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
=============================
Removing a pattern once can create the same pattern again.
Checking the resolved path is safer than filtering the raw input.
Never trust a filename that comes from the user.
